    },
    "q-20536": {
      "dom_id": "step-q20536",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/3d1a01139fdd6f0f9f996cfce43a1e5ad86c2eabe526e9501f72451bd137f9dd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=1$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(0,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=0.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/cfd74974fecdaf62309e089f34f99e0f015584ed3fbb275b9b997c9811c74979.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,0)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(0,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(0,1)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/8faca7d91b813d8ce7a964614a19f29d7e3b20d34cf918463151f37bc8f9614b.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/4127d1311f92f1e03def285cf922bd5235fd4b2a5ca9614f34e7243c9bf79276.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
