    "q-20538": {
      "dom_id": "step-q20538",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/ef23af714377518200c574ba436a30001ed5f60262e11f8157f62d49cbec317e.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are three critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-3,$ $x=-2,\\,$ and $\\,x=-1.$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=-3,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=-1.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,0)$, the point at $x=-1$ must be below the $x$-axis.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,-1)$, the point at $x=-2$ must be higher than the point at $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-3,-2)$, the point at $x=-3$ must be lower than the point at $x=-2.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/66296cbca22d3059fba111935808d1bb0a78e9de31c45948fce207d998c9cfd2.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-3,-2)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-3.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-3,-2)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,-1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/b26af4cdc5af02f2cf0a9a0092f0fe908d70acd428e6a1e24bc848bcc99125e4.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/938c86a415620a2e80873f7cee72b2ebaf5579ae2e67eb966bb1f8836e62c087.png)",
