    },
    "q-81019": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81019",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/2ab398982e30d4b0ce633b77541596f3f5848e1b0a25539037ec3ee0fc557d4d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1.$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\n \n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical line $x=-1$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/7719107149a10c6e5a22e6c343b532f3580582ebd78d3667aad5f2684d2f219d.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,0)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=0$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0).$ Also, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is constant and positive  to the right of $x=0$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing straight line on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/d81008f3d06f41f53a389fa2f36a7b12845fa3dae5a2707a739bbb500c8b4e56.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/763163652b41a7b3cc0b7a6b3faca4799cc073238a50afd9bd3d9c7767c32793.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
